---
import Chapter from '../../components/Chapter.astro';
import SheetIndex from '../../components/SheetIndex.astro';
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import mark from "../../brand/hero-mark.svg?raw";

const sections = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" }, { id: "binding", label: "Binding" }, { id: "unlock", label: "Unlock" },
  { id: "wire", label: "Wire" }, { id: "key-set", label: "Key set" }, { id: "lifecycle", label: "Lifecycle" },
  { id: "rotation", label: "Rotation" }, { id: "rollback", label: "Rollback" }, { id: "deploy", label: "Deploy" },
  { id: "curves", label: "Curves" }, { id: "trust", label: "Trust" }, { id: "gate", label: "Gate" },
  { id: "alerts", label: "Alerts" }, { id: "failure", label: "Failure" },
];
---
<Layout title="Architecture" description="How keystone works: binding, unlock, key sets, their lifecycle, deploy, curves, trust, alerts and failure.">
  <header class="wrap page-head arch-head head-grid">
    <div>
    <p class="folio">Reference</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">How keystone works</h1>
    <p class="deck">keystone speaks the protocol of stock <code>tangd</code> and serves many named key sets from one Cloudflare Worker. Deleting a set's secret revokes it. <code>keyset</code> mints, checks, deploys and revokes the sets.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="head-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={mark} />
  </header>
  <SheetIndex label="Sections" items={sections} numbered />

    <Chapter fig="01-overview">
      <h2 id="overview"><span class="num">01</span> Overview</h2>
      <p>clevis in the host's initramfs talks to keystone through Cloudflare's edge, where a WAF rate limit can sit ahead of the Worker to slow a flood that would otherwise spend the Free plan's daily request quota. A key set is one signing key and one exchange key, held in a Worker secret named <code>TANG_KEY_&lt;NAME&gt;</code>. keystone answers two requests per set: adv returns its signed public keys, and rec runs the key recovery a booting host needs.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="02-binding">
      <h2 id="binding"><span class="num">02</span> Binding</h2>
      <p data-step="1">clevis checks the advertisement's signature against the pinned thp, the thumbprint of the set's signing key, so a forged advertisement fails the bind.</p>
      <p data-step="2">It picks a random c and computes K = c·S from S, the exchange public key; K wraps a new slot passphrase.</p>
      <p data-step="3">The header keeps C = c·G (G is the curve's base point), the kid (the exchange key's thumbprint) and the advertisement, and c and K are discarded.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="03-unlock">
      <h2 id="unlock"><span class="num">03</span> Unlock</h2>
      <p data-step="1">At boot clevis reads C from the header, picks a fresh random e and sends X = C + e·G to rec.</p>
      <p data-step="2">The Worker returns Y = s·X, where s is the exchange private key.</p>
      <p data-step="3">With S from the advertisement stored in the header, the host computes K = Y − e·S = s·C, which equals the bind's c·S because both are s·c·G, and K unwraps the slot passphrase. The Worker sees only the blinded X, never C or K.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="04-wire">
      <h2 id="wire"><span class="num">04</span> On the wire</h2>
      <p>The advertisement, X, Y, and the set name, kid and client address cross the network; the Worker logs one line per successful rec with name, address, ASN and country. C, e, K and the passphrase never leave the host. Since e is fresh each boot, X differs every time. A recording gives up neither C nor K, even to someone holding s: e hides C, and K needs s together with C from the disk's own header.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="05-key-set">
      <h2 id="key-set"><span class="num">05</span> A key set</h2>
      <p>The set <code>server-1</code> lives in the Worker secret <code>TANG_KEY_SERVER_1</code>: the name upper-cased, with <code>-</code> turned into <code>_</code>. Its routes are <code>/server-1/adv</code>, <code>/server-1/adv/&lt;thp&gt;</code>, which answers only for the matching signing thumbprint, and <code>POST /server-1/rec/&lt;kid&gt;</code>. The signing key (ES512 on P-521, ES256 on P-256) signs the advertisement; the exchange key, labelled ECMR for the McCallum-Relyea exchange above, answers rec. Sets share no keys, so revoking one leaves the others serving.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="06-lifecycle">
      <h2 id="lifecycle"><span class="num">06</span> Lifecycle</h2>
      <p data-step="1"><code>keyset new</code> mints a set for the offline key store, <code>deploy --new</code> makes it live, and <code>revoke</code> deletes its secret, so its adv answers 404 and its disks stop unlocking.</p>
      <p data-step="2">The store then keeps null under that name, a tombstone, and every later deploy checks it still answers 404.</p>
      <p data-step="3"><code>keyset</code> refuses new keys under a live name, which would strand every disk bound to it, so rotation uses a new name.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="07-rotation">
      <h2 id="rotation"><span class="num">07</span> Rotation</h2>
      <p data-step="1">The disk's LUKS header holds a passphrase slot and one clevis slot per bound set.</p>
      <p data-step="2">Rotation binds <code>server-2</code> in a second slot, reboots, then unbinds and revokes <code>server-1</code>. Rebooting before the unbind proves the new slot while the old one still opens the disk.</p>
      <p data-step="3">A copy taken before the unbind still opens with <code>server-1</code>'s keys, so the store swaps them for the tombstone.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="08-rollback">
      <h2 id="rollback"><span class="num">08</span> Rollback</h2>
      <p>Each Worker version carries the secrets it was deployed with. After the revoke in version 3, rolling back to version 2 serves <code>server-1</code> again, though the Worker's secret list no longer shows it. <code>keyset deploy</code> flags that through the tombstone as REVOKED-LIVE, and <code>--apply</code> builds version 4 from the current secrets, which drops it. So never roll back across a revoke.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="09-deploy">
      <h2 id="deploy"><span class="num">09</span> keyset deploy</h2>
      <p data-step="1"><code>keyset deploy</code> reads the key store on stdin as one JSON object, a set or null per name, and gives each entry a verdict against the live Worker.</p>
      <p data-step="2">Any REFUSE stops the run with exit 1; without <code>--apply</code> it ends at the plan.</p>
      <p data-step="3">With <code>--apply</code>, wrangler uploads the <code>--new</code> sets and policy edits, then <code>keyset</code> checks each set's signed adv and a real rec, and each tombstone for a 404. The commands are in <a href="/setup/">Install</a>.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="10-curves">
      <h2 id="curves"><span class="num">10</span> Curves</h2>
      <p>Each bar is the measured CPU time for one rec, against the Free plan's 10 ms of CPU per request. P-521, the default and the curve <code>tangd-keygen</code> uses, takes 7 ms at the median and up to 12 ms cold, so it needs Workers Paid. P-256 stays at 6 ms or under and fits the Free plan; <code>keyset new --curve P-256</code> mints one.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="11-trust">
      <h2 id="trust"><span class="num">11</span> Trust</h2>
      <p>Cloudflare, and whoever controls the account, can read the private keys, as can any user or token allowed to deploy a new version, since that code reads the secrets. The API lists secret names only, and disk hosts hold only public keys. To split trust, bind with clevis sss at a threshold of two across keystone and a second Tang server, so neither alone unlocks the disk.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter>
      <h2 id="gate"><span class="num">12</span> Origin gate</h2>
      <p>A set's secret may carry <code>allow</code>, a list of prefixes, and <code>approvers</code>, a list of mail addresses. A rec from an address inside <code>allow</code> is served at once. With no approvers, any other address gets 403. With approvers, its origin, the /24 or /48 it sits in, goes to the Gate, one Durable Object, which opens a pending for that set and origin and mails each approver a link. The link is a token whose MAC, under a key derived from the set's exchange key, names the set, origin and approver. The approval page asks for the set's name before it approves. Approval grants any machine in that origin 10 minutes from the click; a pending left undecided lapses after 12 hours. A set holds 3 pendings at once, and a fourth origin is refused with one notice to the approvers. Origins on Cloudflare's own network, with no ASN, or in 240.0.0.0/4 are never admitted, and a request marked <code>keystone-probe</code> is never served from outside <code>allow</code>. Removing an approver retires their links.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="12-alerts">
      <h2 id="alerts"><span class="num">13</span> Alerts</h2>
      <p>When alerts are set up, each successful rec asks whether its source, a /24 or /48 network plus ASN and country, is new for the set. The first new source in an hour sends one alert; up to five more wait in KV for the hourly digest, and the host never waits on the mail. The kid is public, so an alert shows a request was made; it does not prove an unlock. The Workers log stays the full record.</p>
    </Chapter>

    <Chapter fig="13-failure">
      <h2 id="failure"><span class="num">14</span> Failure</h2>
      <p>If keystone is unreachable or the day's request quota is spent, clevis gets no key and LUKS asks for the passphrase, so losing keystone costs a typed passphrase per disk; the data is untouched as long as each disk keeps its passphrase slot. If a disk is stolen, revoking its set makes rec answer 404, and the disk stays locked from then on.</p>
      <p>Without the <code>GATE</code> binding, a set with only <code>allow</code> still serves its listed origins, a set with approvers refuses every other origin, a valid approval link answers 503, and a probed adv carries <code>keystone-gate: unavailable</code>. If a mail fails, the approver is mailed again on a later request from the held origin, after 5 minutes at first and up to an hour apart, and each send is cut off after 10 seconds. When a pending lapses, the next request from that origin opens a new one and mails a new link; the old link then shows nothing to decide. Revoking the set deletes its secret, so every route, approval links included, answers 404.</p>
    </Chapter>
</Layout>
